WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
 - So we're going to find that there's a number of different ways that we can read through the file,

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.500
 but the most common way that we're going to read through the file is to treat it as a sequence of lines,

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.160
 and we're going to use the determinant loop, the for loop, to do this.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:17.060
 And so what happens here is we get back this handle that opens the file and gives us back the handle.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:22.560
 That handle, xfile, is the variable I named, I just named it xfile.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:26.400
 That's not the data, but it is a sequence.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.660
 It is that file handle represents to Python a sequence

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.900
 that we can potentially walk through and then get all the lines.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.700
 And it's the simplest, most beautiful, elegant way to read all the lines in a file.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.300
 We use the for loop, and we have an iteration variable.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.460
 This is going to take, when we talk about the file, cheese is going to be the first line,

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.400
 then the second line, then the third line, then the fourth line.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.140
 So it's like going through a string, but you're going through a file now,

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.400
 and you're getting it line by line.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.940
 So that's each line.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.540
 I just picked a variable named cheese, so you didn't get confused.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:57.200
 Later I'll call this line.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.000
 But Python doesn't know anything special by naming that variable line.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.660
 Okay, and so this is, it's the for and the in,

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:11.860
 and so I read this as for each line in the file, the file handle xfile.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:16.200
 So run this loop one time for every line, and then print it out.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.660
 So it's actually really quite simple, okay?

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:26.140
 Other languages like C or C++ or other languages, they have to write while loops

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:30.340
 with end of file conditions and all kinds of things that make this very difficult.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:33.900
 But this is one of the prettiest things that Python has.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:39.140
 It's a very, very pretty thing.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:43.640
 Okay, so let's talk about what we might do, and we're going kind of back to iterations now.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.740
 What if we wanted to count the number of lines in a file?

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.100
 Well, this is a basic loop counting pattern.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:54.140
 So we open the file, and then like in all these loops, we do something to sort of prime the loop

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.460
 to get it started, set a variable count to zero, and I'm going to use the variable line

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:04.060
 that's going to go through each of the lines in the file, for line in f hand, down the file.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.640
 And it's going to run this loop once for each line in the file,

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.860
 and the variable line is going to change.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:12.160
 But all I'm going to do is add count equals count plus one, and so that's just,

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:15.040
 like from counters, that's just how you detect.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.260
 So every time we see a line, we're just going to add one to the counter.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.460
 We're not printing the line, we're not even looking at its data at this point.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.700
 And then when the line is done, however many times it has to go, out it comes,

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.940
 and we print out line count equals count.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:31.260
 And so if we open inbox.txt, this is going to do all this work, and then print this line out,

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.740
 and say line count is 132,045.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:39.800
 So this is a little five line program that shows you how to count the lines

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.260
 in a text file using Python.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:48.400
 Again, simple and elegant, and not too much syntax for you to have to learn.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.740
 Now it's also possible to read the file as a series of characters, all in one go.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:54.760
 Read the whole file in.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.560
 Now you've got to be careful, depending on the size of the file,

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:01.340
 this is going to lead to a string variable with a lot of data in it.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:05.600
 Now if it's, you know, 100,000 characters, that's actually kind of a small thing.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.700
 But if it was, you know, 10 million lines, that would probably not be good.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.620
 You'd want to read it one line at a time

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:14.220
 and process each line and then do something.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.740
 But mbox-short.txt is a small little file.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:21.880
 So we open it, and we get back a file object,

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.660
 file handle object, and we call the read method.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.220
 And that says, look, go through and read all the text

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:30.820
 and give it back in one big blob, one big string,

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:32.260
 and I'll put it in imp.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.740
 And so that's where you have a line, a new line,

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.080
 a line, a new line, a line, a new line.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.860
 It's not really lines, it's just a sequence of characters

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.240
 with new lines in there to punctuate them.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.680
 And now you can split that, later we'll see how to split that,

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.100
 into separate lines if you want.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.640
 Now I picked a file that was short,

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.380
 and so this imp variable now has a string in it,

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:56.100
 and I can use the len function,

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.700
 pass a string into the len function,

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.660
 it says, oh, 94,626 characters.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:04.420
 That's kind of a small little file,

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:07.960
 and perfectly okay to read it all in one go.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.500
 And so now I say, just print the first 20 characters,

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.880
 that's beginning to up to but not including 20,

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:17.380
 and so it shows the first 20 characters of that little file

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:19.740
 is a from line, because this is a mailbox file.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:25.140
 Now, let's say we're going to do a searching,

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.740
 and we did this loop where you're looking for something.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:29.720
 And so we're going to search for lines

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.740
 that have a prefix of from.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:33.840
 Okay, that's what we're going to do,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.100
 and we're going to print those lines out.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.020
 So there's lots of lines in this file.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.820
 You know, line, line, line, line, from.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:42.780
 Line, line, line, line, from, right?

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:43.700
 On and on and on.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:45.260
 And we only want to show these lines,

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:46.260
 the ones that match, right?

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.780
 That's what we want to do.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:52.540
 And so we are going to write an open statement,

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:54.340
 and then we're going to loop through,

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:55.620
 and we're going to ask the question,

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.580
 if the line starts with from, print it.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.960
 So sometimes it's going to skip, skip, skip, skip,

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:01.800
 and then it's going to run it,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.900
 and then skip, skip, skip, skip, skip,

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:04.740
 and it's going to run it.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.860
 Skip, skip, skip, and then it's going to run it.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:10.220
 Okay, so that's the basic idea.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:12.220
 And then it'll finish when it's all said and done.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:14.340
 And so this is like a criteria.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:15.840
 This is like a search.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.660
 We're looking for lines that match the string,

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:21.860
 that have the string from as their prefix.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:24.600
 Now, when we look at the output of this,

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.040
 it's kind of weird.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.760
 We see kind of these little blank lines that show up.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.060
 Blank, blank, blank, blank, blank, blank, blank.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:34.760
 What's going on here?

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:36.600
 What's going on?

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:37.820
 So let's take a quick look.

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.900
 The problem is, is new lines.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:45.140
 Well, I mentioned that the file has new lines in them.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:47.320
 And so when you do the for loop,

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:48.860
 it doesn't throw the new lines away.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:51.940
 As you might expect, it would be kind of nice if it did,

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:52.780
 but it doesn't.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.220
 It actually shows you when you read,

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:58.160
 it reads that first line up to and including the new line,

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.000
 and gives you that back as the variable.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.640
 So that is the first new line.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:03.260
 So that means it's going to go down.

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:06.400
 And then the print statement actually adds another new line.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.040
 So that's the second line of the file has a new line

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:09.880
 at the end of it,

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:11.660
 and the print statement adds another new line.

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.260
 So if we take a look at the code,

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:17.180
 there is a new line.

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:18.140
 Oops, come back.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:22.220
 If we take a look at the code,

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:24.780
 this variable line has a new line in it.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:25.620
 Oops, where am I at?

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:26.900
 I'm in the wrong slide.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:27.740
 There we go.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:32.720
 Yeah, this is what I want to do.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:35.120
 If we look at the code, there's a new line in here,

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:36.860
 and then the print adds another new line.

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:39.260
 So the print adds a separate new line.

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:41.260
 And that's how we get two new lines,

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:42.460
 the print statement's new line

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:44.240
 and the new line from the file.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:46.420
 Here's how we fix it.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:47.700
 And you're going to write this code a lot,

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.760
 because when you're reading text files,

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:50.620
 you end up with a new line,

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:52.100
 and often you don't want the new line.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:55.540
 But thankfully, as we saw in the previous chapter,

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:59.020
 there is a nice little function in Python

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:00.780
 for strings called strip

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:02.780
 that allows you to throw away whitespace.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:09.180
 And to review, remember whitespace

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.820
 is anything that doesn't print.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:13.580
 And this new line is not a non-printing character.

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:15.100
 So rstrip gets rid of it.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.020
 So it's a way to get rid of whitespace.

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:19.400
 And rstrip does it from the right end.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:21.980
 So it's the right end of the string.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:26.180
 And so if we just are going to loop

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:27.460
 through all the lines in the file,

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:29.180
 we say line equals line rstrip,

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:31.700
 and then this variable no longer has the new line

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:32.740
 at the end of it.

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:33.820
 We have our little if statement.

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:36.340
 And if we print it, then this line,

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:38.340
 the data has no thing, and then the print,

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:39.860
 the data has no new line in it,

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:41.180
 so the print only goes down one.

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:43.780
 And so now we have single-spaced output.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:45.120
 And so you're going to be doing that a lot.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:47.580
 It's really common to read through a file

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.980
 and then just strip the new line

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:53.380
 or any trailing space off the end of that.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:57.100
 Now, there's a couple of ways to do a loop like this,

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.740
 and let's just think of this as

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:03.540
 we're looking for a line, a file

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.220
 with lots of different lines in it,

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:06.580
 and we want to ignore all the lines

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:08.100
 except some, say, good lines,

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:09.940
 and we want to do something with those good lines,

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:11.420
 or the lines we're looking for.

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:12.380
 Needle in a haystack.

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:15.640
 This is like searching for a needle in the haystack.

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:17.220
 So if you look at this code at high level,

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:18.660
 we're going to loop through everything,

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:21.400
 and then we're sort of picking which lines are,

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:24.060
 and these are the good lines down here.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:26.260
 Now, often we have a bunch more code that we want to do,

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:27.440
 and we're not just printing them,

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:28.740
 but we're going to do a lot of code.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:30.820
 So sometimes you actually structure the loop

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:32.900
 a little bit differently.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:34.120
 And so the way to do it,

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:35.880
 and this is going to do the exact same thing,

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:37.980
 it's just a little different way

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:39.260
 of thinking about this loop.

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:40.940
 So the top part is the same.

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:42.020
 We're stripping it.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:43.480
 And what we're doing here is

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:45.580
 everything's the same here except we add this to not.

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:47.780
 If the line does not start with from,

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:49.700
 that's the translation of that.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:53.020
 If the line does not start with from, continue.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:56.020
 So basically we have a skipping pattern.

00:08:56.020 --> 00:08:59.540
 So the lines we're not interested in, we skip.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:02.500
 So we come down, we skip a lot of lines.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:04.100
 (imitates line skipping)

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:05.700
 And then we find a line that's good,

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:07.140
 and then we fall through.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:08.700
 So this is the good code,

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:10.340
 and then we have all the other good code

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:11.740
 that we want to do to that line.

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:13.580
 We have that showing up down here.

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:16.420
 And so there's just two patterns,

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:19.580
 that are two ways to do the exact same thing.

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:21.720
 So another way to select the lines

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:23.780
 that we're interested in is to use the in operator.

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:27.260
 So we talked before about the in operator and how that works.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:30.900
 So we're basically going to use the continue skipping method.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.060
 So we're going to read all the lines, these first few lines.

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:38.060
 If uct.ac.za is not in the line, skip it.

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:40.940
 And so this is going to print out all the lines

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:45.660
 that have the string uctacza in them.

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:47.740
 And so you see this is the output of the program,

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:48.740
 dot dot dot dot dot.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:53.700
 Sometimes you'll have programs

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.040
 that want to read different files.

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:57.900
 Often I give assignments where I say,

00:09:57.900 --> 00:09:59.900
 show me how this program runs on the short file,

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:01.900
 and then show me again how it runs on the long file,

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:02.860
 just like this.

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:05.260
 And so the way we do that, to input the file name,

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:07.140
 instead of making the file name be a constant

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:11.380
 to the open call, we make the file name be a input.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:13.480
 So we just run an input statement,

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:16.900
 which gives us a prompt, and then we type mbox.txt,

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:18.840
 and then that shows up in this variable F name.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:20.580
 It's the course of string all the time.

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:22.780
 And we pass that in to open, and then we open it,

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:25.500
 and then we do the count operation.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:28.500
 So if we enter nbox.txt, it counts 1797

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:34.860
 subject lines in mbox, and if we give it mbox-short,

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:37.140
 it says there are 27 subject lines in mbox.

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.380
 And again, this is another one of those ifs,

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:41.740
 and it's just counting, but only counting lines

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:44.740
 that match a particular pattern.

00:10:48.020 --> 00:10:50.720
 Okay, so now the user can also type bad file names,

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:52.860
 and we need to be able to deal with that as well.

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:56.860
 And so we're taking a small change to the code.

00:10:56.860 --> 00:11:00.820
 The dangerous code is this line right here.

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:03.060
 This line right here is going to traceback

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:04.500
 if that file doesn't exist.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:05.860
 So what do we do?

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:07.500
 Well, we're going to just expand that.

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:09.260
 The rest of this program is exactly the same.

00:11:09.260 --> 00:11:11.980
 The only thing's different is we've got this line.

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:14.500
 We took out insurance on it,

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:16.300
 and we know that it might blow up,

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:19.880
 and so we have it in a try and accept block.

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:24.180
 So here's how the code runs.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:29.100
 So the input runs, we type in a good file name,

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:30.580
 it comes in here.

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:32.760
 This works, and so it skips the accept,

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:34.960
 and so it runs the code and prints out the count.

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:36.660
 So that's the good pattern.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:41.460
 The bad pattern is here, where we type in a bad file name,

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:45.740
 it comes in the try except, this file name is nanabubu,

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:48.740
 and it's going to blow up, so this line blows up,

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:50.660
 so it jumps down into the accept code,

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:53.960
 prints out, file cannot be opened, so it prints this out.

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:55.900
 Now this quit is really important,

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:57.500
 because if we don't put this quit in here,

00:11:57.500 --> 00:11:58.860
 it's going to continue down here,

00:11:58.860 --> 00:11:59.960
 and that's going to blow up here,

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:03.260
 because file handle is not defined properly at this point.

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:07.140
 And so what we have is we have this quit,

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:09.780
 quit is a special function where it comes in

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:11.100
 and never returns.

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:14.660
 So this is a way to terminate the entire Python program

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:17.060
 silently with no traceback, right?

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:19.200
 So we put in our own error message,

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:20.580
 so we look like we're professional,

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:24.220
 say we could not open this file, and then we stop.

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:25.820
 If you don't, it's going to come down here,

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:29.780
 and it's going to traceback right there, it's going to blow up.

00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:34.780
 So the quit is useful when you want to stop executing,

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:37.720
 because you've detected some kind of an error.

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:40.940
 So that's a quick zoom through opening

00:12:40.940 --> 00:12:43.500
 and reading through files and doing some patterns.

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:47.460
 Most of the rest of the programs in this course

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:52.460
 are going to say open for rstrip,

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:55.500
 look for, and then do something interesting.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:57.540
 That's going to be our loop that we're going to do

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:00.060
 over and over and over again.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:03.500
 And now we see how there's looping, and if,

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:08.220
 and iteration, and variables are starting to come together,

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:10.220
 and you can actually sort of do a program

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:12.180
 that does something useful.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:15.100
 But, before we get to too many more programs,

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:17.140
 we got to switch a little bit, switch gears,

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:19.620
 and talk up next about data structures,

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:21.700
 and that is the shape of data,

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:25.780
 and how we can use more intricate and complex variables

00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:27.860
 to help solve our problems.

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:30.440
